WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:06.680
We are once again faced with the combination
of ignorance and arrogance from Donald Trump

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:13.520
and it's always a perfect storm of what I
call weaponized ignorance around the economy.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.840
Specifically, remember when Donald Trump called
now criminal national security advisor, Michael

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.230
Flynn in the middle of the night to ask him,
hey, is a strong dollar or a weak dollar better

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.220
for the American economy?

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:33.270
Donald Trump dealing with the economy is sort
of like you realize your car needs, the transmission

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.750
rebuilt and you bring it to a guy who only
knows how to diagnose whether the car is out

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.170
of gas or not, and often even diagnosis that
incorrectly.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.190
You're not even going to be able to assess
the transmission with the guy who can only

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.610
read the gas, uh, gas reading.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.150
Uh, and that is similar to what is going on
with Donald Trump and the American economy.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:58.510
Maybe a better analogy is your engine blows
and you bring the car to a bakery.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.550
The Baker is equivalent to Donald Trump dealing
with the economy.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.280
It was a signal when Trump called Michael
Flynn and said, is a strong dollar or a weak

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.580
dollar good that this is not a guy who knows
anything about the economy and Donald Trump

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:19.700
tweeting yesterday, quote Jay Powell and the
Federal Reserve fail again, no guts, no sense,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.510
no vision, a terrible communicator.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.350
So to really do this correctly, we've got
to go through it sequentially.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:31.200
Donald Trump has continued to just rage over
the idea that the economy would be doing way

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.960
better today if the Federal Reserve would
help them out with more and bigger federal

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.570
funds, rate cuts in the way that Trump believes
they've quote helped out previous presidents.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.140
Now Donald Trump has gotten two rate cuts
recently.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.510
I believe it was August 1st he got a 25 basis
point cut around August 1st.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.310
Don't, don't, don't remember the exact date.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.250
And then you've got another 25 basis point
cut yesterday.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.130
25 basis points is like 0.25%.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.280
But Trump is insisting it's just not enough.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.229
And the truth is that both increases and decreases
to the federal funds rate.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:10.969
Can quote help a president because the logic
is if you cut the federal funds rate, it makes

00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:16.099
it cheaper for banks to borrow money and that
can conceivably stimulate economic activity

00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:17.909
and that will boost the stock market.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:22.099
And it will give something to Donald Trump
to brag about how great the economy is doing.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:27.950
But when the federal funds rate is increased,
it can also help the economy in that the Fed

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:33.879
must see the economy as being strong enough
to tolerate a rate increase signaling that

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:35.290
things must be going pretty well.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:41.639
So, first of all, it's not even cut and dry
that only rate cuts help a president, uh,

00:02:41.639 --> 00:02:45.269
look better in terms of the economy that they
are stewards of.

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:49.579
But to analyze this outlandish behavior by
Donald Trump, you also have to dig deeper

00:02:49.579 --> 00:02:55.249
about in the modern economy, does a rate cut
even really do the things that we historically

00:02:55.249 --> 00:02:58.019
believed rate cuts would do?

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:04.030
And the answer is only sort of the, the mechanism
that is imagined for a rate cut to help the

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.829
economy is the Federal Reserve cuts the rate.

00:03:08.829 --> 00:03:14.219
This leads to Americans buying more durable
goods like cars and stuff like that.

00:03:14.219 --> 00:03:19.689
Interest rates drop on mortgages, so people
will buy houses, uh, they will refinance mortgages

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:23.800
and save money and have some extra money every
month to spend on other stuff.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:30.040
But the reality is that as the economy continues
to modernize, the uh, reality is a federal

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.170
funds rate cut of 25 or 50 basis points is
not going to have that significant an effect.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:39.339
People aren't saving up waiting for a rate
cut and then they go out and they get a car

00:03:39.339 --> 00:03:43.870
and a house and refinance spending has much
more of a natural cycle that's related to

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.919
a where in life people are and and uh, other
factors.

00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:51.540
And in addition to that, you've got to remember
that the bottom half of the economy has had

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.839
stagnant wages for like 40 years.

00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:59.089
So at 25 basis point cut or a 50 basis point
cut or a 75 basis point cut is not going to

00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:01.219
stimulate any significant amount of spending.

00:04:01.219 --> 00:04:03.949
For a lot of those people, they just don't
have any money.

00:04:03.949 --> 00:04:09.610
So the long and short of it is that even this
reliance on rate cuts to quote jumpstart an

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.339
economy or to prevent the recession, it's
old thinking.

00:04:13.339 --> 00:04:18.750
It's of course based on the sort of economic
status quo that has not been good for half

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:19.750
of the country.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.470
Anyway, that's a bigger conversation.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.750
And of course Donald Trump is furious, not
only about only getting another 25 basis points.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:30.840
He's furious that the Fed is predicting GDP
growth of only 2.2% this year.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:37.730
Remember he was promising three, four, I think
he even talked about 5% GDP growth under his

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:38.730
presidency.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:39.730
Of course, it's not happening.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:46.090
It was never going to happen and sadly the
Trump stuff around this is a really big distraction

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:49.500
from some bigger economic questions that we
don't have answers to.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.780
Trump is flat out wrong about the federal
funds rate.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:58.390
Trump is attacking the federal, uh, the Federal
Reserve chief that he selected, Jerome Powell.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:04.530
I mean, it's crazy, but it's a distraction
from what happens to the economy if we don't

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.580
deal with climate change isn't an economy
that is based on infinite growth on a planet

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.770
of limited resources.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.270
Fundamentally a mathematical impossibility.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.520
How good is an economy really?

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.930
Even if the stock market is high or unemployment
is low, if half the country can't afford an

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:30.140
unexpected $400 expense or is one illness
away from bankruptcy, there are really important

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.170
questions to solve about the economy.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.730
Some of which we're going to talk with progressive
economists, Dean Baker later on today, which

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.410
I really look forward to.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:42.130
Donald Trump doesn't even understand the problems,
nevermind potential solutions.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.130
And that's probably the biggest tragedy of
all of this.

